<Base title="Measurement Methodology and Sources | CupToGram" description="Learn how CupToGram estimates ingredient weights and why measurements can vary." path="/methodology/"><main class="wrap content"><h1>Measurement methodology</h1><p>Ingredient conversion values are reference estimates, not exact physical constants. They can vary with brand, particle size, moisture, temperature, settling, and the technique used to fill a measuring cup. CupToGram's current reference weights use the US customary cup convention where noted.</p><h2>Primary reference</h2><p>Most currently published ingredient weights have been checked against the <a href="https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/learn/ingredient-weight-chart" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">King Arthur Baking Ingredient Weight Chart</a>. The source provides ingredient-specific gram equivalents and recommends weighing ingredients for best accuracy. For entries marked as verified, the source and review date are stored with the ingredient data.</p><p>The uncooked white rice value is still provisional because it has not yet been checked against a primary source. It is marked unverified in the data and should be reviewed before relying on it for precision cooking.</p><h2>How to use the values</h2><ul><li>For flour and fine powders, fluff first, spoon into the cup, and level without packing unless the recipe says otherwise.</li><li>For brown sugar, follow the recipe's packing instruction.</li><li>For liquids, use a liquid measuring jug on a level surface.</li><li>For baking precision, prefer grams measured with a kitchen scale.</li></ul><h2>Measurement variation</h2><p>Displayed ranges are practical estimates of how measuring technique and ingredient conditions may change a result; they are not laboratory-certified tolerances. Do not treat these estimates as nutrition, medical, or food-safety advice.</p></main></Base>
